PR #32  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Matrix multiply-accumulate instructions must trap on vstart ≠ 0 because their inner dot-product reduction across W packed sub-elements cannot be cleanly partitioned at SEW boundaries for interrupt/resume; hardware or emulators that ignore this silently corrupt accumulator state on trap return.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/vector.h:174-175  __vstate_csr_restore
      Blind csrw CSR_VSTART during context restore without checking whether the interrupted instruction is a non-restartable wide-reduction (e.g., matrix multiply-accumulate). If a wide-reduction instruction trapped with vstart ≠ 0, restoring vstart causes the instruction to resume from a misaligned element boundary, recomputing partial dot-products while the prior partial-accumulation state was lost in microarchitectural state across the trap boundary. The spec requires vstart=0 as a precondition for such instructions, but no check is made here.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/vector.h:132-132  __vstate_csr_save
      Correctly saves CSR_VSTART into dest->vstart during context save. This is necessary to preserve the architectural vstart index for proper context save/restore. The issue is not saving vstart but restoring it blindly.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/ptrace.c:241-241  riscv_vr_set
      ptrace SETREGSET(NT_RISCV_VECTOR) copies attacker-supplied vstart into vstate->vstart with zero validation. The invalid_ptrace_v_csr() function validates vtype, vl, vcsr, and vlenb but entirely omits any check on vstart. A malicious debugger can inject vstart ≠ 0, which will later be blindly written to CSR_VSTART by __vstate_csr_restore, causing accumulator corruption when a non-restartable wide-reduction instruction resumes at a bogus element index.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/vector.h:266-266  __riscv_v_vstate_restore
      Calls __vstate_csr_restore(restore_from) which blindly writes CSR_VSTART without checking instruction restartability. This is the intermediate layer that feeds the vulnerable csrw CSR_VSTART in every vector state restore path (context switch, signal return, kernel-mode vector restore).
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/vector.h:315-322  riscv_v_vstate_restore
      Top-level vector state restore API. Calls __riscv_v_vstate_restore (which calls __vstate_csr_restore, which blindly csrw CSR_VSTART). No check or mitigation for non-restartable wide-reduction instructions. This is the entry point for all context-switch and return-to-userspace restore paths. The only guard is riscv_v_vstate_query (state != OFF), which is unrelated to instruction restartability.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/entry-common.h:19-19  arch_exit_to_user_mode_prepare
      Deferred vector restore path on return to userspace. Calls riscv_v_vstate_restore which ultimately restores vstart blindly. If a wide-reduction instruction was interrupted by a signal or preemption, the restored vstart ≠ 0 will cause accumulator corruption on resume.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/kernel_mode_vector.c:183-183  riscv_v_context_nesting_end
      Kernel-mode vector context restore when returning from a nested interrupt/preemption to a kernel-mode vector user. Calls __riscv_v_vstate_restore which restores vstart blindly. If a kernel-mode vector context was executing a wide-reduction instruction when preempted, the restored vstart ≠ 0 will corrupt the accumulator.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/ptrace.c:118-118  riscv_vr_get
      Read-only GETREGSET(NT_RISCV_VECTOR) path that exports vstate->vstart to userspace debugger. This is a read operation with no modification of CSR state, and is correct behavior for debugger inspection of saved context.
  [OK] arch/riscv/lib/uaccess_vector.S:58-59  __asm_vector_usercopy_sum_enabled
      Legitimate vstart consumer: reads CSR_VSTART on vector store fault to compute remaining bytes in scalar fallback path (iNum -= vstart). This is the correct and intended use of vstart — the hardware sets vstart to the element index where the fault occurred, and the fault handler uses it to determine how many bytes were successfully written before retrying the remainder in scalar code. This is unrelated to the blind-restore vulnerability.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:455-455  
      Constant definition: #define CSR_VSTART 0x8. This defines the architectural CSR number for vstart. Purely a constant with no logic implications.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/uapi/asm/ptrace.h:100-101  
      UAPI struct field declaration: unsigned long vstart in struct __riscv_v_ext_state. This is a data structure definition for signal/ptrace vector context. The field itself is architecturally necessary — vstart must be saved/restored as part of vector context. The vulnerability is in how this field is written to CSR_VSTART, not in its existence.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/uapi/asm/ptrace.h:114-115  
      UAPI struct field declaration: unsigned long vstart in struct __riscv_v_regset_state. Same rationale as above — this is the regset-facing view of vstart exposed via NT_RISCV_VECTOR. The struct definition is not the source of the vulnerability.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/vector.c:106-108  riscv_v_insn_is_vector
      Instruction classification for emulation: checks if CSR operand of a SYSTEM opcode is in range [CSR_VSTART, CSR_VCSR] or [CSR_VL, CSR_VLENB] to classify it as vector-related. Correct behavior for instruction emulation dispatch — this identifies instructions that access vector CSRs and need vector-aware emulation.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_vector.c:108-109  kvm_riscv_vcpu_vector_get_reg
      KVM get-reg handler resolves KVM_REG_RISCV_VECTOR_CSR_REG(vstart) to &cntx->vector.vstart. This is a read-only path for the ONE_REG interface that returns a pointer to the guest context vstart field. Used for VM state export (migration, debugging). No CSR modification occurs here.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:1122-1123  num_vector_regs
      Counts vstart as one of 5 vector CSRs plus 32 vector registers (=37 total) for ONE_REG enumeration. Pure accounting for the KVM register list size.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:1137-1137  copy_vector_reg_indices
      Iterates over 5 vector CSRs starting with vstart (index 0) for ONE_REG index export. The loop exports KVM_REG IDs to userspace so that userspace knows which registers are available. This is metadata export, not state manipulation.
  [OK] tools/testing/selftests/riscv/vector/validate_v_ptrace.c:84-84  ptrace_v_early_debug
      Selftest: declares volatile vstart variable for CSR readback via inline asm. This is a test-only declaration, not production kernel code.
  [OK] tools/testing/selftests/riscv/vector/validate_v_ptrace.c:111-112  ptrace_v_early_debug
      Selftest: reads vstart CSR via inline asm csrr in the tracee process. This is a test that reads the architectural CSR for comparison with ptrace-exported value. Read-only test code.
  [OK] tools/testing/selftests/riscv/vector/validate_v_ptrace.c:143-143  ptrace_v_early_debug
      Selftest: declares vstart_csr for ptrace PEEKDATA comparison in the tracer. Test infrastructure variable.
  [OK] tools/testing/selftests/riscv/vector/validate_v_ptrace.c:171-172  ptrace_v_early_debug
      Selftest: peeks vstart via ptrace PTRACE_PEEKDATA in the tracer. This reads the tracee's vstart variable via ptrace data access for comparison testing. Read-only test validation.

── opensbi ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #32  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Matrix multiply-accumulate instructions must trap on vstart ≠ 0 because their inner dot-product reduction across W packed sub-elements cannot be cleanly partitioned at SEW boundaries for interrupt/resume; hardware or emulators that ignore this silently corrupt accumulator state on trap return.
  [BUG] firmware/fw_base.S:672-690  _trap_handler
      The trap entry/exit path saves only GPRs (TRAP_SAVE_GENERAL_REGS_EXCEPT_SP_T0) plus mepc/mstatus (TRAP_SAVE_MEPC_MSTATUS) and trap_info (TRAP_SAVE_INFO). No CSR_VSTART, CSR_VL, CSR_VTYPE, CSR_VLENB, or vector register state is saved or cleared. After the C handler returns, TRAP_RESTORE_MEPC_MSTATUS + TRAP_RESTORE_A0_T0 + mret resumes execution with vstart retaining its pre-trap value. If a matrix multiply-accumulate instruction (vmmacc.vv et al.) was interrupted mid-execution at vstart=k (0<k<vl), the instruction resumes from element k, recomputing partial dot-products from a misaligned sub-element boundary. The fused inner-product reduction spanning W packed sub-elements cannot be partitioned at arbitrary architectural element boundaries — the intermediate partial sum is microarchitectural state lost across the trap boundary. Standard trap return via mret silently preserves stale vstart, enabling silent data corruption.
  [BUG] firmware/fw_base.S:694-724  _trap_handler_hyp
